---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import DialogueRunner from '../../components/DialogueRunner.astro';
import scene from '../../data/dialogue-drive.json';
---
<BaseLayout title="STATIC — Interactive dialogue">
  <section class="px-6 pt-16 pb-8">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl">
      <a href="/game#interactive-dialogue" class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-dim transition-colors hover:text-violet-bright">&larr; back to STATIC</a>
      <p class="eyebrow mt-5 mb-3">CH 02 · Playable scene</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-5xl font-semibold uppercase tracking-tight text-ink md:text-6xl">{scene.meta.title}</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-ink-dim">
        Two main characters, Ian and Saoirse are talking in the car after Ian picked her up 
        on the road after Saoirse's car accident. They have not seen each other in ten years.
        The atmosphere in the car is awkward and tense. You are in control of the conversation.
        Your choices set flags that change later lines and gate later
        options. Four endings in all. Click to advance; click again to skip the type-on.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  
<section class="px-6 pb-24">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl">
      <DialogueRunner scene={scene} />
      <p class="mt-8 text-ink-dim">
        <strong class="text-ink">Design note:</strong> This scene is a compact demo of the
        dialogue system: persistent flags, gated choices, escalating reactions, and a hidden
        ending that rewards restraint. Choices made in the opening beats change what Saoirse
        says, and what Ian is allowed to say, at the end. In the full game, scenes like this
        run deeper and feed a persistent story state: what you say in the car follows you
        for the rest of the investigation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
